Goals:
-----

 - Develop a treatment of CPU and GPU versions of the same code that:
 
     1. Allows maintenance of a single code base in a shared Rp 
     namespace 


     2. Avoid the need to create different parallel sets of classes
     in distinct Rpc and Rpg namespaces. 

     3. Create a manageable system that allows for maintaining object
     files and unit tests for several compiled versions of the code 
     simultaneously.

Comments: Goal 1 has been largely accomplished b current system of class
templates in Rp, with "Types" classes as template parameters. This file
proposes a way to accomplish goal 2, and avoid the need for distinct
Rpc and Rpg namespaces.  

Discussion
----------

The most basic problem in maintaining CPU and GPU versions of the same
code arises from the need to create CPU and GPU versions of the classes
that store fields that are defined on a mesh and functions that manipulate 
them. Relevant mathematical operations include things like binary and
in-place (compound) arithmetic operations, exponentiation, and FFTs.

Strategies for specializing the behavior for CPUs and GPUs can distinguish
them either a compile time (via polymorphism) or inheritance (via 
templates). 

A. Run time polymorphism via inheritance and virtual functions:

Create an interface / base class for each field class and subclasses 
designed to use CPU and GPU memory.  This must rely on the use of virtual 
functions to distinguish behavior, and so requires that any mathematical 
operation that is performed on a field be implemented by a virtual 
functions.

Comments:

   - In virtual functions that implement operations for which the output
     is a field, and one or more other fields may be provided as inputs,
     it is natural to call the member function on the field that will 
     hold the output, since there the output is always unique.

   - To create generic code, functions that take fields as parameters
     must take instances of the base class. Operations are actually only
     allowed for input and output fields of the same type, however, so
     there must be some type of run-time check of some sort of label
     to ensure that all fields involved in an operation are of the same
     type (i.e., same mesh dimensions and the same CPU or GPU backend).

B. Compile time polymorphism via templates.

This is the proposal that is examined in detail in the remainder of this
file. In this proposal, we define class templates that take some sort of
template parameter that identifies the backend. Specifically, to take
advantage of analogies with the existing implementation, the proposal
is to define classes that essentially all take two template parameters:
1) The integer dimension of space D=1,2, or 3, and 2) A class or
enumeration that is uniquely identified with a particular backend. I
propose to us a classes for this purpose so that the classes can contain
additional information, e.g., about associated used by the associated
FFT library for real and complex types (which are different for FFTW 
and for cuFFT). 

In this approach, we would define a base class template for RField as

template <int D, class T> 
class Template {};

and then define partial specializations in which T is taken to be 
either T=CpuTypes for CPU code or T=CudaTypes for GPU code written in 
Cuda. 

Class templates with the same two template parameters would also be 
written for a variety of other higher level classes that own or interact
with field classes. A class template Parent<D,T> for a parent class that
owns a field can then have a member Field<D,T>. In this manner we can
create a hierarchy of templates that own each other, such that all of
the templates used in a given executable would have the same values for
both D and T.

Comments:

  - Mathematical operations could be implemented as global functions
    (for vector and reduction operations) or as member functions of
    and associated FFT<D,T> class for FFT operations, as done 
    currently. It appears that no changes would need to be made to 
    any of the existing functions, and that it would remain easy to
    add new mathematical operations without adding additional 
    virtual functions to the field classes whenever you find it
    convenient to define a new mathematical operation. 

  - Check that all fields or arrays involved in a mathematical 
    operations must use the same backend would be naturally imposed by 
    the C++ type system, rather than as additional run-time-checks.
    The data types for fields are specializations of templates in
    which one of the template parmeters identifies the backend, so
    the backend is embedded in the type definitions. Functions would
    we defined for allowed operations, involving fields that use the
    same backed, but not for prohibited operations, and the compiler
    will choose the correct version of overloaded functions.

  - I need to think about how to implement unit tests, but it may
    be natural to define some of these as member function templates
    that take the backend types class T as a template argument. 

Thoughts:

  - I think the template approach is actually a rather a natural 
    conceptual fit to a problem, but more awkard to implement in
    C++ because of pecularities of the language. In practice, a
    single backend will always be used throughout execution of 
    a given program, and could be chosen simply be calling a 
    different variant of the executable. We don't need the 
    run-time flexibility provided by run-time polymorphism. 

  - The strongest argument for preferring compile-time polymorhpisms 
    is really that the C++ mechanisms for working with templates in
    a large framework are simply much clumsier than mechanisms for 
    working with a system based on inheritances. The awkwardness of 
    patterns of file inclusion for C++ templates is really a defect 
    of the language that is a result of its descent from C, and 
    the use of the C model for importing modules in which 
    information about other classes is imported by a text preprocessor 
    that literally pastes header files into other files.  The 
    resulting awkardness of large template-based systems in C++ 
    is real, however, and can provide good reasons to favor 
    run-time polymorphisms even for problems in which compile-time 
    polymorphism via templates might otherwise seem like a natural 
    choice. 

  - The current version of PSCF uses a system of templates that
    is in some ways very similar to that proposed here, in which
    all of the classes defined in the Rp name space already have
    two class templates analogous to the two proposed here. The
    template based proposal would allow re-use of all of the 
    existing code for basic mathematical operations, without
    modification, and provides a path to modify class templates
    to improve the design without ever breaking the code. 

  - The template-based system proposed here might or might not 
    be the best option if we were designing a new package from
    scratch - I can see argument either way. It does, however,
    seems to be the best option, and perhaps the only feasible
    one, for improving the design of the current system without
    starting over. 

Outline (order of operations)
-----------------------------

Non-disruptive  changes to existing code, in preparation:

1)  Change classes in Rp that use several class template parameters so as 
    to use a single Types class template parameter (essential for later 
    transforming role of the Types class).

2)  Define a enumeration that identifies backends and make a value of it
    a static member of the existing Types classes, so that code that takes
    a Types class as a template argument can ask explicitly what type of
    backend is being used at run time. 

3)  Try to develop abstractions to treat device <-> copy operations that
    are required in for some Gpu implementations but not for the analogous
    CPU versions, in order to further reduce the number of functions that
    need different implementations for Cpu and Gpu code. The less 
    specialization exists in the existing code, the easier it will be
    to transform to a purely template based design.

Disruptive changes:

1)  Create Pscf::CpuTypes by copying Pscf::Rpc::Types and create 
    Pscf::CudaTypes by copying Pscf::Rpg::Types.

    At this stage, these two new classes in Pscf will include forward 
    declarations of classes in Rpc and Rpg and aliases for classes 
    defined in these namespaces. This is intended to be a temporary
    state - the process described will allow us to later remove all
    of these references to program-level namespaces.  Because the 
    existing Types class header files contains only forward 
    declarations and aliases, but don't include other header files
    from Rpc or Rpg, these header should be usuable anywhere in Pscf.

    Add Complex and Real type aliases to these classes, to represent
    the data types used by the associated FFT classes.
 
2)  Change references to Rpc::Types to CpuTypes and Rpg::Types to 
    CudaTypes throughout Rpc and Rpg namespaces, respectively. Also
    change file names for the relevant header file includes.

    (All code should compile again at this point. Changes have thus 
    far been superficial changes in class names and namespaces in which
     they are defined).

3)  Create templates for RField, RFieldDft, CField etc. in Prdc

    - Create empty base class templates in Prdc of the form
      class RField<int D, class T> where T is a "*Types" class.

    - Process: Make explicit partial specializations of RField, 
      RFieldDft, CField, FFT, etc. that are just renamed versions of 
      existing classes now in Prdc::Cpu and Prdc::Cuda. Each of these
      partial specializations will still have D as an unspecified
      template parameter, like the existing classes.

    - Idea: Use template parameter rather than Cpu and Cuda namespace 
      qualifiers to distinguish names of Cpu and Gpu variants of 
      analogous classes.  For example: 

         Prdc::Cpu::RField<int D> => Prdc::RField<int D, CpuTypes>
 
    - Advantage: Template parameters in the class template of a 
      parent object can be used in type definitions for member
      variables and function interfaces.  Note: Namespaces cannot 
      be used as template parameters, so we have to use a class or
      enumeration as template parameter to identify a backend.

    - Note: No change necessary to functions defined in VecOp and 
      Reduce. These functions operate on DArray or DeviceArray
      containers, which are base classes for the CPU and GPU field 
      containers, respectively.

4)  Make any other required changes in Prdc namespace to get this 
    namespace level directory to compile. Look for direct reference 
    to, e.g., RField<D> and FFT<D> template specializations and 
    replace these by RField<D,T> and FFT<D,T> etc.

5)  Repeat change in class template names for definitions of all 
    prdc unit tests. At this point code and unit tests in src/prdc 
    should compiles and run successfully. 

6)  Change aliases to Fields and FFT types in CpuTypes and CudaTypes. 
    This will immediately allow re-use throughout the class templates 
    in Rp.

7)  Search for and replace direct references to field and FFT type names in
    Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

    (Code in rpc and rpg should compiles again at this point, but not 
    unit tests)

8)  Find and replace direct references to Field<D> and FFT<D> types 
    in rpc and rpg unit tests with, Field<D,T> and FFT<D,T>

    (All code and unit tests should again compile and run)

9)  Change references via "typename T::RField" etc to more explicit 
    RField<T,D> within class templates in the Rp namespace. This should 
    cause no change in behavior, and is a first step towards removing 
    need for a long list of aliases in the Types classes.

10) Change names of any remaining class templates in the Rp namespace 
    require explicit specialization of subclasses in the Rpc and Rpg 
    namespaces by adding name "Base" to the base class template name.  
    Distinguishing names of base and derived classes will prevent later 
    clashes between base and derived class templates when base and 
    derived classes are all defined in the Rp namespace.

11) Go through the following operation for class templates defined in Rp
    for which aliases exist in the Types classes, class by class:

    For each class Rp::Class :

       - Replace definitions of subclass templates Rpc::Class<D> 
         and Rpg::Class<D> for each class by explicit instantiations of 
         Rp::Class<D, CpuTypes> and Rp::Class<D, GpuTypes>. For the 
         moment, the required explicit instantiation declarations and
         definitions can be done within existing files in the rpc/ and 
         rpg/ directories, by just changing class template and 
         namespace names. 

       - After each such conversion of a subclass to an explicitly
         instantiated template specialization, change the corresponding 
         alias in the corresponding Types class. (Everything should 
         compile again).

       - Replaced all references in Rp namespace via "typename T::Class" 
         to Class<D, T> in existing templates in the Rp namespace, then 
         remove the associated alias from the Types class. (Everything 
         should still compile).

       - Replace references to Class<D> to Class<D,CpuTypes> in unit 
         tests.

    At the end of this process, the Types classes will contain almost 
    no aliases, except those for Real and Complex types used by FFT 
    library. Each Types classes will have been gradually transformed 
    from a container for a long lists of classes defined in Rpc or Rpg 
    into much simpler identifiers for "policy" classes for a backend.

12) For each class that is defined in Rpc or Rpg but for which no alias
    is defined in the Types class, repeat the process applied above for
    classes that are referred to via aliases defined in the Types classes.
    The remaining classes will primarily be classes that implement
    specific algorithms (bd steps, mc moves, compressors, analyzers, etc), 
    which are subclasses of base classes for which an aliases is defined
    in the current Types class. 

    At the end of this process, their will not be any classes defined 
    in the Rpc or Rpg namespaces, and these namespaces will cease to
    exist.

